WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.213
Some underground equipment and portable equipment

00:07.213 --> 00:09.737
gets its power through a trailing cable:

00:09.737 --> 00:12.475
a heavy, flexible cable that runs from a power

00:12.475 --> 00:14.838
center to the machine and moves with it.

00:14.838 --> 00:16.825
Your instructor will show you which equipment at

00:16.825 --> 00:19.825
your mine is cable-powered. Whatever the machine,

00:19.825 --> 00:22.812
the cable is part of it. If the cable is damaged,

00:22.812 --> 00:25.738
the machine isn't safe, and neither is anyone who

00:25.738 --> 00:29.250
steps on, grabs or drives over that cable.

00:30.372 --> 00:33.497
Inside the jacket are the power conductors and,

00:33.497 --> 00:35.972
in most cables, a ground conductor.

00:35.972 --> 00:38.597
The jacket and the insulation are the only things

00:38.597 --> 00:41.410
between you and the voltage. A cut,

00:41.410 --> 00:44.472
a crush or a bad splice can bring the energized

00:44.472 --> 00:47.385
conductor to the surface, where a wet glove or

00:47.385 --> 00:49.010
a wet floor will find it.

00:52.330 --> 00:54.805
Look at the cable as part of your pre-shift check,

00:54.805 --> 00:57.417
and whenever you move the machine. Look for cuts

00:57.417 --> 01:01.167
or gouges in the jacket, flattened or kinked sections,

01:01.167 --> 01:04.517
exposed conductors, and tape wrapped over damage.

01:04.517 --> 01:07.617
Tape is not a repair. Check where the cable attaches

01:07.617 --> 01:10.105
to the machine. The standard requires it to be

01:10.105 --> 01:13.105
attached in a way that protects the cable and keeps

01:13.105 --> 01:15.542
strain off the electrical connections.

01:15.542 --> 01:18.242
If anything looks wrong, don't energize it.

01:18.242 --> 01:20.867
Tell your supervisor, and write it up on the pre-trip

01:20.867 --> 01:22.742
inspection list or app.

01:23.872 --> 01:26.459
Splices are where cables most often fail.

01:26.459 --> 01:31.797
Under 30 CFR 57.12013, a permanent splice has to

01:31.797 --> 01:34.947
be mechanically strong, insulated at least as well

01:34.947 --> 01:37.597
as the original and sealed against moisture,

01:37.597 --> 01:40.109
with damage protection as close as possible to

01:40.109 --> 01:45.384
the original. Under 57.12088, no splice except

01:45.384 --> 01:48.434
a vulcanized splice or its equivalent is made within

01:48.434 --> 01:51.484
25 feet of the machine, unless the machine has

01:51.484 --> 01:54.234
a cable reel or similar payout system.

01:54.234 --> 01:56.922
A temporary splice is allowed only to move the

01:56.922 --> 02:00.597
equipment for repair. Splicing is electrical work.

02:00.597 --> 02:03.122
If you're not qualified, your job is to recognize

02:03.122 --> 02:05.135
a bad splice and report it.

02:06.250 --> 02:11.200
Under 57.12005, mobile equipment doesn't run over

02:11.200 --> 02:13.863
power conductors, and loads aren't dragged over

02:13.863 --> 02:16.613
them, unless the conductors are properly bridged

02:16.613 --> 02:19.388
or protected. Underground, that means watching

02:19.388 --> 02:21.713
for cables when you're tramming a loader or driving

02:21.713 --> 02:25.213
a truck, especially around corners and in dust.

02:25.213 --> 02:27.888
If a cable is in your path and it isn't bridged,

02:27.888 --> 02:31.025
stop and get it moved or protected the right way.

02:32.140 --> 02:37.940
Under 57.12014, energized cables above 150 volts

02:37.940 --> 02:40.927
phase-to-ground aren't moved with equipment unless

02:40.927 --> 02:43.740
insulated sleds or slings are used.

02:43.740 --> 02:46.540
When they're moved by hand, insulated hooks,

02:46.540 --> 02:49.965
tongs, ropes or slings are used, unless people

02:49.965 --> 02:52.227
are protected some other suitable way.

02:52.227 --> 02:54.165
Your instructor will show you the tools your mine

02:54.165 --> 02:57.915
uses. The safest choice is to have the cable deenergized

02:57.915 --> 02:59.890
and locked out before it's handled.

03:01.006 --> 03:04.443
Extra cable for shovels, cranes and similar equipment

03:04.443 --> 03:06.568
is stored on a reel on the machine,

03:06.568 --> 03:10.131
in a cable boat, or otherwise protected from damage.

03:10.131 --> 03:12.418
A loose coil on the floor gets run over,

03:12.418 --> 03:13.768
crushed and cut.

03:14.921 --> 03:17.333
Here's a scenario. Near the end of a shift you

03:17.333 --> 03:19.908
notice a small nick in a cable jacket,

03:19.908 --> 03:22.283
and you can see something shiny underneath.

03:22.283 --> 03:24.958
Someone says to wrap some tape around it and finish

03:24.958 --> 03:27.533
up. What do you do? Don't energize it,

03:27.533 --> 03:30.396
and don't tape it. Report it, tag the machine out

03:30.396 --> 03:32.471
of service the way your mine requires,

03:32.471 --> 03:35.296
and let a qualified person make a proper repair.

03:35.296 --> 03:38.096
A small nick in a dry heading becomes a shock hazard

03:38.096 --> 03:40.108
the first time the floor is wet.

03:41.242 --> 03:43.904
Another one. A rock has rolled off the rib and

03:43.904 --> 03:46.754
pinned a cable. Don't yank the cable out and don't

03:46.754 --> 03:49.167
pry the rock off with your hands on the cable.

03:49.167 --> 03:51.767
The cable may be crushed inside, and the rib that

03:51.767 --> 03:54.817
dropped one rock may drop another. Get the power

03:54.817 --> 03:57.929
off and locked out, have the area examined,

03:57.929 --> 04:00.104
and report the cable for inspection before it's

04:00.104 --> 04:01.079
used again.

04:04.424 --> 04:06.662
Here's ground fault in plain words.

04:06.662 --> 04:09.212
If damaged insulation lets current leak out of

04:09.212 --> 04:11.787
a conductor and onto the machine's frame,

04:11.787 --> 04:14.287
that current needs a safe path back.

04:14.287 --> 04:16.787
The ground conductor gives it one, so protective

04:16.787 --> 04:19.575
devices can sense the problem and shut the power

04:19.575 --> 04:22.412
off before a person becomes the path instead.

04:22.412 --> 04:27.187
That's why 57.12027 requires frame grounding,

04:27.187 --> 04:29.587
or equivalent protection, for mobile equipment

04:29.587 --> 04:34.612
powered through trailing cables, and 57.12003 requires

04:34.612 --> 04:36.837
overload or short-circuit protection for those

04:36.837 --> 04:37.749
cables.

04:38.870 --> 04:41.120
When protection trips, it's telling you something

04:41.120 --> 04:44.183
is wrong. Don't keep resetting it, and never bypass

04:44.183 --> 04:46.995
it, jump it or swap in a bigger fuse.

04:46.995 --> 04:50.220
Report it, and let qualified people find the fault.

04:50.220 --> 04:53.582
From 2021 through our latest data, inspectors cited

04:53.582 --> 04:57.120
overload protection, controls, protected conductors

04:57.120 --> 04:59.795
and uncorrected dangerous conditions six times

04:59.795 --> 05:02.757
at Dix River, and grounding tests three times at

05:02.757 --> 05:07.907
Boonesboro. Under 57.12030, when a potentially

05:07.907 --> 05:10.782
dangerous condition is found, it's corrected before

05:10.782 --> 05:13.795
the equipment or wiring is energized.

05:14.912 --> 05:18.387
Inspect the cable every shift. Tape isn't a repair.

05:18.387 --> 05:21.037
Don't drive over cables unless they're bridged.

05:21.037 --> 05:24.300
Handle energized cable only with insulated tools,

05:24.300 --> 05:27.475
or better, deenergize it first. And when protection

05:27.475 --> 05:30.162
trips, report it. Never defeat it.

05:31.274 --> 05:34.437
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

05:34.437 --> 05:35.937
override this video.
